WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.250
Remember when Monday mornings

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.370
were like this?

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.410
They're a bit different now.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.890
While those of us lucky to be working

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.010
are now doing so from a spare bedroom

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.810
or a kitchen table, the
Coronavirus has forced

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:38.290
a sudden and mostly successful pivot

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.730
to working away from the office.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.090
While the circumstances are unwelcome,

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:46.130
there are some benefits
to this style of working.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.970
You have a more content workforce,

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.410
a more motivated workforce,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.730
and also a more productive and
efficient workforce as well.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.130
So when the Coronavirus is behind us,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.690
will our workday ever be the same again?

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.450
We spend a lot of our
time traveling to work

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.810
and for most, the
commute is getting longer

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.490
all over the world.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.850
Globally, two fifths of
professionals consider

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.890
the commute to be the
worst part of their day.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.330
Commuting has been found
to be a major cause

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:26.930
for stress that impacts on
our physiological health

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.770
and as well, wellbeing.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.450
The total working day gets longer,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.290
you get less time at home,

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.570
you do less exercises when
you have long commutes,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.810
you also cook less healthy food.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.370
I followed Swedish couples
over a 10-year period

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.010
and we found that commuting
long distances to work

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.810
over one hour increases
the risk of separation.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.690
Overall we find that it's
a 40% increase

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.410
of risk of separating.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.250
Before the pandemic,
approximately 25 million

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.850
U.S. workers spent more than 90 minutes

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.890
getting to and from their jobs every day.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.890
In South Korea, one in four workers

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.090
has a journey that long.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.330
One of those people is Park Jong Han,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.450
a manager at a major telecoms company.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.210
Basically, I throw away
three hours of my daytime

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.530
just to get to work and
to come back home.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.250
Since February, SK Telecom

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.930
has instructed its employees,
including Jong Han,

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.090
to work from home, or if suitable,

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.010
to work from smaller, local offices

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.090
in the surrounding neighborhoods of Seoul.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.490
One of the mobile offices, if I walk,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.250
it takes about 15 minutes.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.650
I truly appreciate the time,

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.690
and it actually comes back
to motivation to work harder.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.930
It's not just time, it's very
quality time that I spend

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.370
instead of you know,
getting stuck in a bus,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.170
stuck in traffic somewhere,
you cannot compare.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.970
The amount of time that
I spent with my family

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.610
really increased, you know?

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.650
During lunchtime, I can
have lunchtime with my son.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.050
Have you just shortened your hours

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.530
and you're able to go and, like you said,

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.810
exercise more or do you find yourself

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.690
working more as a result?

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.250
Because it's been too long,
I totally forgot about it,

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:21.570
but in the very beginning
when this program started,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.050
I was exhausted actually,
during the first few days

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:29.370
because there's no place for rest, right?

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.770
Because I need to work anywhere,

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:37.210
wherever I go, my mind was always on work

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:40.610
and when I talked to the teammates,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.530
they actually felt the
same, in the beginning.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.810
But after a few weeks, I think everyone found

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.690
their own way to work

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.850
and now I know how to adjust, you know,

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:56.410
myself into, you know, work
mode versus rest mode and so on.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:00.770
So losing that journey into work

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.370
could be good for us.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.490
But research claims our
commute can also provide us

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.530
with a means to separate our personal

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.370
and professional lives

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:12.010
And in a world of 10-second

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.770
bed-to-laptop commutes,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.970
that's a chance for some me time.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.450
But while journey times were
already generally on the up,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.530
more of us were actually
starting to commute less

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.770
thanks to a relatively
new trend:

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.810
Flexible working.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.170
Increasingly we have seen
people remote working

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.810
from lots of different locations.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.770
There has been a reluctance
to encourage flexible working,

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.530
perhaps because managers
see it as a loss of control,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.090
it can be quite difficult to manage people

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.490
who you don't necessarily
see on a day to day basis,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.450
there might be concerns around efficiency

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.970
or productivity.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:54.770
And is that a bit of a myth,

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.130
or is any of that born out in fact?

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.330
What we found is that productivity

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:04.130
can actually improve as a
result of flexible working

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.050
and there isn't necessarily any impact

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.170
upon the business, if
it's managed effectively.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.010
There's quite a lot of benefits.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:14.770
There is an opportunity for workers

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:18.690
to have more autonomy
over their scheduling,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.170
over when they work and where they work

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.250
and when people have
that degree of autonomy,

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.770
it does lead to a
workforce that is happier.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:28.610
You may have flexibility over the time

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.490
that you have to go into work or the time

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.770
that you can come home so
that you're able to avoid

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.770
rush hour traffic and if
you're working from home

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.010
a few days a week or completely at home,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:44.690
then obviously you can avoid
the commuting altogether

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.290
and there's also benefits for employers.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.010
A study by Harvard and New York University

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.050
found that for those working from home,

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.610
the workday is 48 minutes longer,

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.050
probably replacing that
time you spent commuting.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:00.490
There's significant
benefits for organizations

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.690
that are able to manage these
kind of processes effectively.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.770
So what actions can
organizations implement

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.010
to harness the power of flexible working?

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.850
They need to have very clear
processes and procedures

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.730
around operating flexible work

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.370
so that everybody has
the same expectations.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:22.290
One department allows the
majority of their employees

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.410
to work flexibly and another department

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.890
which is led by another line manager

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:30.450
who has a different
view, then you might find

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.850
that flexible working
isn't as supported as much.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:36.410
You need to treat people consistently

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.130
and in a fair way but you
also need to recognize

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:41.690
that there can be individual differences

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:45.330
in how people adapt to flexible working.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.210
It will suit some people but not others,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.010
so you do need to have
a degree of flexibility

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:55.330
yourself as a manager
in how you manage teams

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:56.730
and how you manage people.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:03.130
And this flexibility can
lead to productivity.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.570
A two-year Stanford
study of 1,000 employees

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.450
at one company found that working

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.290
from a home office resulted in

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.330
a 13% increase in
productivity

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:16.410
and 50% of them were
less likely to quit.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.330
Despite this, half of them
still wanted to go back

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:21.330
to the office nine months later,

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.130
even though their average
commute was 40 minutes each way.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.610
Another survey conducted
by Bain and Company

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.570
on its own employees, found
that productivity increased

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.370
for some thanks to no
commute and an ability

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:37.050
to focus better at home, but
also decreased for others

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.250
due to a lack of work mindset

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:41.530
and a dedicated workspace.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:43.850
So perhaps we'll start
to see a more hybrid style

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:46.690
become the norm, where for
some days we work from home

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:48.490
for specific solo tasks

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:49.930
and others we travel to the office

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:52.930
to meet and collaborate with the team.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.370
The changing nature of our working lives

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.610
has already led to many
white-collar workers

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.410
leaving the city or moving further out,

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.850
to find more space, some greenery,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.050
or to escape the urban beeps and bustle.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.970
One person who misses the
commute is Alice Shay,

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:11.570
an urban planner and designer.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:14.450
I miss riding the subway.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:17.890
Riding the subway is one of
those incredible experiences

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:19.370
in New York City, it's got its highs

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:22.290
and it's got its lows, but honestly,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.810
the subway system in New York City

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.730
is one of the greatest levers
for equity that we have.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.770
It is one price to get all
the way across the city.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.810
Many of us are now working
from home, you know,

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.970
or at least for a couple
of days of the week.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.210
Will where we live drastically
change, do you think?

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:41.770
We're in a moment where
commutes can be zero,

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:43.930
so what does that mean
for how we are distributed

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:48.410
across our settlements
and urban agglomerations?

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.010
I think it provides a lot of flexibility

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.330
for certain workers, it's true,

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.890
but there's other ties
that keep people to place,

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:56.970
right, it's not just about commute,

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:59.490
so that's one factor amongst many.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:02.530
Access to services, access to family,

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:06.370
access to the culture
that keeps the city alive

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.450
or your lifestyle interesting.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.970
So the city as we know it
isn't exactly going anywhere,

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.490
but it might change in
the way that we use it

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:16.730
and the way it works?

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:19.610
Our street infrastructure and streetscapes

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.730
can be reconfigured in a
time of social distancing

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:24.650
when we're understanding that private cars

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.890
may not be as essential
as we thought they were.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:31.210
We posited that if you took
the streetscape in Manhattan,

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:35.610
which is equivalent to four
times the size of Central Park,

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.410
reconfigured it in a
way that actually looked

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.730
at streetscapes as being
about space for mobility

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:44.410
and distribution at a bigger scale,

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:47.010
so how do we actually
decrease the number of cars

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.690
and what are the opportunities
that come with that?

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.930
So increased space for
pedestrians and walking,

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:55.570
more effective distribution systems,

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.490
buses could run at least twice as fast

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:01.770
getting people around the
city in an equitable way.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.090
The transformation of the way
that we use our streetscapes

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.930
could also enable better
delivery of urban systems.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:13.170
Is there an opportunity to
build more equality in cities,

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:14.650
you know, key workers for instance

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:17.170
who aren't doing jobs
on a laptop right now,

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.210
they have to be in
those areas, don't they,

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:20.370
you know, no matter what?

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.490
I mean you bring up a great point.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:25.090
Service workers, essential workers,

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:27.090
they are geographically bound.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.490
Mobile healthcare could be
distributed more widely,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.210
more equitably, particularly
if there's government

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:36.730
and public drivers behind the
way that that is distributed.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:38.890
We're also seeing massive
changes in the way

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:40.570
that retail is happening, right?

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.450
Many, many shops are closed.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:47.450
When we have times when
cities are more porous,

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:49.290
when there's flux, when you know,

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.650
the real estate sector isn't so saturated,

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.890
that porousness allows for an opportunity

00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:01.730
for microentrepreneurship
and also new innovations

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:03.890
at a kind of small scale.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.290
You know, small businesses
that are starting up,

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:08.570
food trucks that are
gonna be distributing food

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.450
across the city, different
types of services

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:13.770
that operate in different types of places.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:17.450
Our changing relationship with work

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:19.410
could affect where we live, too.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:21.610
It could accelerate a move to what's known

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.330
in urban planning theory
as the polycentric city.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:27.410
Polycentric city would be a place

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:30.290
where you can work, you can live,

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.690
you can recreate, you can
have your social life,

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:36.370
your family in a more
local and distributed way.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:38.810
In cities like Paris,

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:41.130
it's known as the 15-minute city,

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.730
where daily necessities are
within a 15-minute reach

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:45.290
on foot or by bike.

00:11:45.770 --> 00:11:49.490
You reduce transit times,
you reduce GHG emissions

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.810
and also you provide more equitable,

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:53.570
more sustainable access to services

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.850
by this more distributed city model,

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:58.010
but that's not to say that you need

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:59.530
to live in the suburbs, right?

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.330
So what's to say the office

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:04.370
can't come closer to us?

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:07.410
I think going forward, companies will need

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.890
to support workers in that externality

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.290
and it could take many forms.

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:13.930
Does everyone wanna work from home?

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.410
No, some people like going to a place,

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.490
commuting, how are companies
that usually would say

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:22.410
come to our main office, oh now actually

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:24.850
we have a set of
sponsored co-working spaces

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:26.970
across the city that are
closer to where you live

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:31.250
and can decrease your
commute and maybe you go

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.450
to those spaces three days a week

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:34.970
and you come into the office two.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:37.170
I think we're gonna see a whole range

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.610
of new types of work as
people have gained confidence

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:44.570
in the efficiency of work
from home during this time.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:50.530
Since the virus outbreak,

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.650
serviced office brand
Regus has already seen

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:55.290
a surge of more than 40% in activity

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:58.450
in New York City commuter
hub southern Connecticut.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:00.930
In the U.K., house builders
are seeing developments

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:02.930
outside of London driven by a change

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.690
in home office working.

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:08.690
While experiences and jobs
vary all over the world,

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:11.210
many workers have come to expect change.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:14.130
More than 90% of people in a recent survey

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:15.850
said they wouldn't return to the office

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:17.730
full time after COVID.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.970
There's reasons for employers
to embrace the change too.

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:22.770
Some already are.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.570
Twitter and Facebook have said the switch

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:28.610
could become permanent for
large parts of their workforce.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.970
The COVID-19 pandemic
has sent shock waves

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:32.730
through the world of work.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:35.290
Having an on-off
relationship with the office

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:38.050
could make us happier
and more productive,

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.930
while also helping the environment

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.930
and making our cities more livable.

